<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger Williams</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1603–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger Williams was an English Puritan minister banished from the Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1635 for his views on religious liberty and native land rights. In 1636 he founded Providence on land obtained from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a>, and he secured a charter for Rhode Island, which separated church and state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-williams.jpg" alt="Roger Williams" width="320" height="652" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roger_Williams_statue_by_Franklin_Simmons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Banished from the Massachusetts Bay Colony, <b>Roger Williams</b> founded Providence in 1636 on land obtained from the Narragansett.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li><li><blockquote>Dissenters were expelled: <b>Roger Williams</b> and Anne Hutchinson went to what became the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations.</blockquote><a href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-rhode-island">Providence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1655" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roger Williams"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1655">What was here in 1655? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Boast not proud English, of thy birth and blood Thy Brother Indian is by birth as Good. Of one blood God made Him, and Thee, and All. As wise, as faire, as strong, as personall.&#8221;</p><footer>A Key into the Language of America (1643), chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/63701/pg63701-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;An enforced uniformity of religion throughout a nation or civil state, confounds the civil and religious, denies the principles of Christianity and civility, and that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh.&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is the will and command of God that, since the coming of his Son the Lord Jesus, a permission of the most Paganish, Jewish, Turkish, or anti-christian consciences and worships be granted to all men in all nations and countries…&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution for Cause of Conscience (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;God requireth not an uniformity of religion to be enacted and enforced in any civil state; which enforced uniformity, sooner or later, is the greatest occasion of civil war, ravishing of conscience…&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution for Cause of Conscience (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when they have opened a gap in the hedge, or wall of separation, between the garden of the church and the wilderness of the world, God hath ever broke down the wall itself, removed the candlestick, &amp;c.&#8221;</p><footer>Mr. Cotton's Letter Examined and Answered (1644) &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
